A||||3||theogre||Alldata & Pop Mech ||||1||
Z||000000||theogre||04-01-2000||01:20 AM||||I recently got to look at an Alldata disk. It's perfectly scary how much information is there. It was just as interesting what wasn't there.<P>Just for reference. A full set of Alldata now comes on 5 DVDROM disks, it is/was about 15 CDROMs. Those "in the biz" considering DVD should be aware that DVDROM does have problems. Some are Win9x issues. Some are hardware/media issues. In short, DVDROM Is Not yet as reliable as CDROM.<P>1. Who has the Alldata/pop mechanics single car CD? Did you also get the TSB's? What did they charge for the TSB add on? ((All users of this disk please respond.))<P>2. If you did not get the TSB's what data was there? Most of what I saw on the full set for Fiero is TSB data. Even the Full Set doesn't have the complete service manual for any year Fiero or any other car. <P>Even the full set could not simply search for specific documents. In many cases a TSB references another TSB or GM document. There is no way to just look up that refered document. <P>In the test case a TSB on air conditioning refers to another GM doc from jan 97 that outlines specific GM aproved R134a conversion methods. If the referenced document was there then ther was no way to simply search for it. (The refered document was GM Corp Service Bulletin # 43-12-07E from 01-97)<P>Overall I found the full version Alldata disk lacking major portions of critical data. While I was only able to really exersice the GM disk, I can't imagine the others are signifigantly differant.<P>It was very good that there is a source of TSB data besides a GM dealer. It was rather alarming that the disk left out huge amounts of other vehicle specific data. <P>Moreover much of the data that is available is confusing without the complete manual. For example the TSBs Alldata had put on the disk included revisions to the Helms published manuals. Some of the updates can be used on their own, others are potentially dangerous if used so. <P>Other TSBs are simply annoying. There are TSBs on the disk that are nothing but GM telling dealers when tools will ship or that a new book will ship or is available from Helms. Even then there are updates to the updated book that make no sense at all without the original master book. ((Example: There is a "master" book for TH125c. Then there is an update book that include changes up to publication. Then on there are TSBs that update that. The TSBs often do not include all the surounding data, just the small data to correct the book. A book you likely don't have even as a professional.))||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierospeeder||04-01-2000||03:04 AM||||I dont have the fiero one, but i do have the popular mechanics ford tbird turbo version.<BR>The tsb is about 12 bucks if i remember.  <BR>On the tbird, it supplies complaints and problems with the car.  Updated fixes on what should be done to fix it.  One was leakage of the trans fittings.  Another was where to get the entire electrical connectors set if you ever break one.  Its just another way of them to make money.  If im not doing anything, i might look on my disk.  There is a way to get TSB without paying for it, i think i figured out how to get the code.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fformula88||04-01-2000||11:08 PM||||You may be able to get the TSB's from the dealer.  If you go to the alldata website and get the TSB titles and #'s, then you can find which TSB's you really want.  Many listed for the Fiero are for general repairs, not specific Fiero issues.  Take the #'s you want to your friendly Pontiac dealer and ask if you can get copies of them.  The Ford dealer I go to for my Probe let me copy the TSB's for it.  They led me to a desk with all their TSB books, and offered me the service of their copy machine.  It was very nice of them, and it didn't take too long.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||04-02-2000||12:48 AM||||This was ment to be a review of the Alldata product. Basically it's a usefull source of TSB's but not much else. Many TSB's are useless without the shop book.<P>Just as annoying you can't simply search the Alldata product for a specific document by number or text. There are hundreds even thousands of TSB's per car & manufacturor.<P>For what the full subscription costs. the software is kind of lame.<P>You can get the list of TSB's and Recalls, with sumaries, for any car sold in the U.S. at <A HREF="http://www.nhtsa.gov/" TARGET=_blank>NHTSA</A> along with a large amount of other info.||||reg||1
